.TH XENIX 4 "2026-10-09" "Substrate" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
xenix \- Xenix execution personality
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B Xenix
personality
.RB ( PERS_XENIX )
runs Xenix programs: every executable in the segmented
.B x.out
format described in
.BR xout286 (4),
whichever processor it was built for.
.PP
Xenix has two system call interfaces.  8086 and 80286 images are 16-bit
segmented programs and trap through
.BR "int $5" ;
80386 images are 32-bit and use the System V
.B "lcall $7,$0"
gate.  They are one personality all the same, in two halves, because they
are one system: a Xenix/386 installation keeps all three kinds of binary in
a single tree, and a program of one kind runs another without knowing which
it is.  The x.out header selects the loader, and the process's word size
selects the half of the personality that handles its traps and signals.
(Until October 2026 the 16-bit half was a personality of its own,
.BR SCO-X/286 ,
rooted at
.IR /perso/xenix286s .)
.SH THE 32-BIT HALF
A Xenix/386 program is linked for one flat address space: text at address
0, data at 0x01880000, and the stack growing down from the start of the
data.  Its code and data selectors (0x3f and 0x47) are separate LDT entries
with the same base, and the loader puts each segment at the address its
segment table entry gives.  The program is told where its break is in
.B EAX
at entry.
.PP
A system call is
.B "lcall $7,$0"
with the number in
.B EAX
(the
.B cxenix
sub-function in
.BR AH )
and the arguments on the stack above the stub's return address.  Carry
reports failure with the errno in
.BR EAX .
A second result is returned in
.BR EDX :
the parent's pid from
.BR getpid ,
the effective id from
.BR getuid " and " getgid ,
the status from
.BR wait ,
the write end from
.BR pipe ,
and from
.B fork
zero in the parent and non-zero in the child.
.PP
.B signal
is given the address of libc's return trampoline in
.BR EDX ,
and a handler returns through it with
.BR "lcall $0xf,$0" .
The System V.3 variants that share the call number
.RB ( sigset ", " sighold ", " sigrelse ", " sigignore ", " sigpause )
are implemented.
.PP
The calls implemented are those of the 16-bit half less
.BR mount ", " umount ", " stime ", " utime ", " acct
and the Xenix
.BR nap ", " locking
and semaphore and shared-data calls; an unimplemented call fails with
.BR EINVAL .
The Bourne shell from both SCO Xenix 386 2.2.3 and 2.3.4 runs, with pipes,
command substitution, redirection, traps, and children of either word
size, as do
.BR ls ", " id " and " uname .
.PP
The rest of this page describes the 16-bit half.
.SH SYSTEM CALL INTERFACE
A Xenix/286 program traps with
.BR "int $5" .
Substrate leaves IDT vector 5 at DPL 0, so the instruction faults with
.B #GP
before it can reach the
.B #BR
handler; the personality catches that fault, confirms the faulting bytes are
.BR "CD 05" ,
and emulates the call.
.PP
The register convention, taken from the Xenix crt0's
.B __syscall
thunk at text offset 2, is:
.TP
.B AX
Call number.  The low byte is the System V call; the high byte is a
sub-function, used by call 40 (the Xenix multiplexer, which carries
.BR brkctl ,
.BR ftime ,
.BR nap ,
.BR locking ,
.BR rdchk ,
.BR chsize ,
the shared-data and IPC calls) and by call 57
.RB ( utssys :
0 is
.BR uname ,
2 is
.BR ustat ).
.TP
.BR BX ", " CX ", " SI ", " DI
Arguments one through four, one word each.  A long is split across two
consecutive slots, low half first.  A pointer is an offset in
.BR DS .
This is the convention of the small-data models (small and middle).
.TP
.BR BX " (large-data models)"
A program of the large model
.RB ( XE_LDATA ,
.BR "cc \-Ml" ;
Xenix/286 has no compact model, its compiler taking only
.BR \-Ms ,
.B \-Mm
and
.BR \-Ml )
pushes the arguments as for any C function and traps with
.B BX
pointing at the first of them, in the stack segment.  They are words in
the same order, except that a pointer is two: its offset, then its
selector.  The personality reads the block into the same four arguments,
so that a call is implemented once; a table of which arguments of which
call are pointers tells it where the two-word ones are.
.TP
.B AX
On return, the result \(em or, when the carry flag is set, the positive
.BR errno .
.TP
.B BX
On return, the high half of a 32-bit result.  The Xenix stubs move it to
.B DX
to build the
.B DX:AX
that Microsoft C returns longs in.  Calls that yield two values use the same
pair:
.B getpid
returns pid in AX and ppid in BX,
.B getuid
returns uid and euid,
.B pipe
returns both descriptors, and
.B wait
returns the pid in AX and the status in BX.
.PP
.BR fork (2)
is the exception to the AX rule: it returns the
.I other
process's pid in AX, and the child is told apart by
.B BX
being zero.  The child does not re-enter the trap handler, so its BX is
staged into the trap frame before the fork and the parent's is restored
after.
.SH TRANSLATED INTERFACES
.IP \(bu 2
.BR open (2)
flags.  Xenix uses the System V values \(em
.B O_NDELAY
is 0004,
.B O_CREAT
0400,
.B O_TRUNC
01000,
.B O_EXCL
02000 \(em where Substrate uses the Linux ones.  The same translation applies
to
.B fcntl(F_GETFL)
and
.BR F_SETFL .
.IP \(bu
.BR ioctl (2).
The Xenix termio group
.RB ( TCGETA
= 0x5401) sits one lower than Substrate's, which reserves 0x5401\(en0x5404
for the termios family Xenix has no equivalent of.  The 17-byte
.B struct termio
is converted to and from
.BR "struct termios" ,
including the System V aliasing of
.B VMIN
and
.B VTIME
onto
.B VEOF
and
.BR VEOL .
.IP \(bu
.BR stat (2)
and
.BR fstat (2)
report the 30-byte Xenix
.B struct stat
(16-bit device, inode, mode, link count, uid, gid and rdev; 32-bit size and
times).
.IP \(bu
.BR read (2)
on a directory.  Xenix/286 has no
.BR getdents (2):
a directory is read with
.BR read (2)
and returns 16-byte V7
.B struct direct
records.  Substrate synthesizes them, which is what makes
.B ls
list a directory and
.BR ttyname (3)
find the terminal by scanning
.IR /dev .
.IP \(bu
Signal numbers.  Xenix and Substrate both descend from V7 but extended it
differently above SIGTERM, and at 7 and 10.
.IP \(bu
.BR brkctl (2),
the segmented
.BR sbrk (2).
DGROUP's break is the process break; a far data segment carries its break in
its own descriptor limit, and
.B BR_NEWSEG
allocates a fresh LDT slot with a 64 KiB window behind it.
.SH SIGNALS
Delivery is V7 style, which is what Xenix/286 is.  The handler's far pointer
is pushed as a far-call frame on the program's own 16-bit stack:
.IP
.nf
SP+4   signal number   (the handler's int argument)
SP+2   interrupted CS  \\  the return address, so the handler's
SP+0   interrupted IP  /  lret resumes the interrupted instruction
.fi
.PP
There is no trampoline and no saved register block, because V7 had none: the
handler is an ordinary C function, so it preserves what the ABI requires and
everything else is understood to be clobbered.  The disposition reverts to
.B SIG_DFL
as the handler is entered, and the handler re-arms it.
.SH FILESYSTEM NAMESPACE
The personality's path prefix is
.IR /perso/xenix .
An absolute path is resolved there first and then against the root
filesystem, so a Xenix
.I /perso/xenix/bin/sh
wins over the native one.
.PP
For everything except
.BR execve (2),
the bare-path fallback refuses Substrate-native
.RB ( ELFOSABI_SUBSTRATE )
objects, because pulling one into a process that stays Xenix would have its
Substrate-numbered syscalls misrouted by this personality.
.BR execve (2)
is exempt: it replaces the image outright and the loader that claims the new
file sets the personality with it, so a Xenix program can exec a Substrate
binary and simply becomes a native process.  Both directions work \(em
Microsoft Word execs
.I /bin/sh
into Substrate's shell, which execs a Xenix
.I SetWrdKeyMap
straight back into this personality.
.SH DEBUGGING
.TP
.B debug=perso:x286:xout
Trace image loading: processor, run-time flags, entry segment, segment count,
and the selectors and offsets the process is entered with.
.TP
.B debug=perso:x286:syscall
Trace each emulated call as it returns, with its arguments and result.
.TP
.B debug=perso:x286:entry
Also trace each call on the way in.  This is what identifies a program parked
in a blocking call, which the return-side trace cannot show.
.SH KNOWN LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
The shared-data
.RB ( sdget ,
.BR sdenter ,
.BR sdleave ,
.BR sdgetv ,
.BR sdwaitv ),
semaphore
.RB ( creatsem ,
.BR waitsem ,
.BR sigsem )
and System V IPC sub-functions of call 40 return
.BR ENOSYS .
.IP \(bu
.BR fcntl (2)
record locks
.RB ( F_GETLK ,
.BR F_SETLK ,
.BR F_SETLKW )
work, with two things to know.  The C library does not send them by the
.B fcntl
call but as modes 5, 6 and 7 of the Xenix
.B locking
call, with a far pointer to the
.B struct flock
whatever the program's model; that is where the personality takes them,
translating the structure and Xenix's own
.I l_type
values.  And substrate keeps record locks on the open file, not on the
file: a lock is seen by the processes that share the descriptor it was
set through (a child, after
.BR fork (2)),
and not by one that opened the file itself.  The modes of
.BR locking (S)
proper, 0 to 4, are accepted and lock nothing.
.IP \(bu
.BR ptrace (2),
.BR profil (2)
and
.BR uadmin (2)
are not implemented.
.IP \(bu
.BR utssys (2)
implements only
.BR uname ;
.B ustat
returns
.BR ENOSYS .
.IP \(bu
In a large-data program
.RB ( XE_LDATA )
the pointers
.I inside
what an argument points at are far only where the personality has been
taught so: the
.I argv
and
.I envp
arrays of
.BR execve (2).
The System V IPC calls are not implemented in any model.
.SH VERIFIED
SCO Xenix/286
.BR echo ,
.BR cat ,
.B ls -l
(including the V7 directory-read path),
.BR sync ,
.B csh
and
.B vi
(middle model, full screen) run correctly, as does cross-personality
.BR execve (2)
in both directions.  A 37-command sample of the distribution media runs
clean: 37 ok, 0 crash, 0 noload.
.PP
The shipped
.B cc
compiles and links end to end, with correct floating point \(em these
binaries carry no x87 instructions, the linker having rewritten each into a
two-byte software interrupt, and the loader patches them back.  Microsoft
Word 3.0 for Xenix reaches its full editing screen under
.BR TERM=ansi .
.PP
Xenix/86 images
.RB ( x_cpu
0x04) run under this same personality: they are protected-mode images
carrying the same LDT selectors as an 80286 one, and the 8086 instruction
set is a strict subset.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/perso_xenix.c
The personality, both halves.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/perso_sysv386.c
The 32-bit entry convention, signal frame and calls, which UNIX System
V/386 has too; see
.BR svr4 (4).
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/xenix/xenix286_syscalls.h
Call numbers and call-40 sub-functions, read out of the SCO Xenix 286
Development System's own libc.
